#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541115 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b5785c-9c86-58fa-8702-7b20eaf94717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540600} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d02e90-1b24-51db-a87e-21fe5d5fa85e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540804} {:answer/id #uuid "da8619fc-53ce-586e-8d07-73efa17d3b93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540896} {:answer/id #uuid "de3f6a49-2e44-56e2-9f7d-ad3b02678d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540906} {:answer/id #uuid "f746d0ee-4d45-5138-a444-dd8fb7c426b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de3f6a49-2e44-56e2-9f7d-ad3b02678d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540906} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa476148-4520-535e-8de8-c555998b437f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e0b25f6-0e44-56a6-a167-f47173302a18" :question/math-academy-id "q-113672" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{4/5}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{4}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{9/5}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{9/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{5}{x}^{9/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{4/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36064afd-4624-50aa-b9f7-869ddad593ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540613} {:answer/id #uuid "3e30535b-824e-5d84-96a9-2fa046e67302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540632} {:answer/id #uuid "576b7268-d013-5459-8484-febaf9a3d59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540675} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5946b6-1628-5af6-a597-d8bfff766bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540905} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd73e85-3ada-52db-9939-f4f819c081ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc5946b6-1628-5af6-a597-d8bfff766bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540905} :answer-field/id #uuid "889a0978-0193-5e72-b84c-66f0c08de47a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6915e10-1543-50b0-a358-2c56a1366444" :question/math-academy-id "q-336744" :question/problem "A fermentation tank initially holds $180\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved nutrient.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved nutrient is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of nutrient $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{18})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{18})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which minerals enter the tank minus the rate at which minerals leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which minerals enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of minerals enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *minerals* enter the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which minerals exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which minerals leave the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n180+20t-30t=(180-10t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of minerals in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *minerals* leave at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{180-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{3Q}{18-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q=0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(18-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|18-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(18-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(18-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(18-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{18-t}Q) & =(18-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\ (18-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(18-t)^{-4}Q & =0.8(18-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3}) & =0.8(18-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(18-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.8(18-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(18-t)^{-3} & =0.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(18-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.4(18-t)+C(18-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of minerals. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ C(18-0)^{3}+0.4(18-0) & =5 \\\\ C(18)^{3}+7.2 & =5 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{2.2}{{18}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.4(18-t)-\\frac{2.2}{{18}^{3}}(18-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.4\\cdot 18\\,\\left(\\frac{18-t}{18}\\right)-2.2{\\left(\\frac{18-t}{18}\\right)}^{3} \\\\  & =7.2\\,(1-\\frac{t}{18})-2.2{(1-\\frac{t}{18})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =7.2,\\beta =-2.2,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =7.2-(-2.2)=9.4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c33caad-bb2f-5503-bf90-b0887fffc7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540549} {:answer/id #uuid "395b2c98-0606-52cd-ad1e-4a590c5bc289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4{x}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540620} {:answer/id #uuid "58bcf515-bc8d-5c7f-a87c-e1d6836d3944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540678} {:answer/id #uuid "720da65f-7b8d-5025-8be5-6124aff10c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540724} {:answer/id #uuid "9358fcee-68fe-5474-97b4-702d3a35e3e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{x}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c33caad-bb2f-5503-bf90-b0887fffc7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540549} :answer-field/id #uuid "df1e8bd8-c60e-5022-8cc3-6497fe7a88f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "daa61eb1-b03b-51c0-8270-14c708ae1e5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-29643" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}={x}^{-5}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-5$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5+1}}{-5+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4}}{-4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "174968af-4f97-5fc2-96c1-946a1318679f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545243} {:answer/id #uuid "1787098a-f4be-583e-8b74-371cf41e0f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545244} {:answer/id #uuid "1f6a6925-66ac-5529-a6b8-a5006b6b8998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545260} {:answer/id #uuid "260a6d7a-9e0e-525f-a529-7e99511654a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545281} {:answer/id #uuid "81d4dc11-5639-5bc7-9888-4db8339d8ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d4dc11-5639-5bc7-9888-4db8339d8ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545440} :answer-field/id #uuid "825ca9e2-d349-54be-8ff8-677e119c487a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48d8f1b2-776f-5613-8b52-d9e267acd25f" :question/math-academy-id "q-56116" :question/problem "Find $a$ if ${a}^{2}-6=6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the ${a}^{2}$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2}-6 & =6 \\\\ {a}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{a}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\ |a| & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\ |a| & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3} \\\\ |a| & =\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ |a| & =2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ a & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "000f3d81-c5d0-5946-b172-3bf3fe65444a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\pm 2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545200} {:answer/id #uuid "34ac2f0d-2f64-5eae-b276-d1d7570929cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545305} {:answer/id #uuid "43f62726-6db5-59d8-a65d-6482ea48a157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545328} {:answer/id #uuid "492c1b78-ce03-55b1-9771-7be25a941eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\pm 2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545338} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba5b039-42c1-5f88-a8de-65c4f49d3c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "000f3d81-c5d0-5946-b172-3bf3fe65444a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\pm 2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545200} :answer-field/id #uuid "74304a95-1479-50c8-b9fd-11f3f0128771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9a2ec726-00b7-5cc7-85a6-cb62e4711dd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-12796" :question/problem "Find $a$ if ${a}^{2}=40.$" :question/worked-solution "We take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{a}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\ |a| & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 10} \\\\ |a| & =\\sqrt{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{10} \\\\ |a| & =2\\sqrt{10} \\\\ a & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "363dd3dd-0402-51c3-b564-d0f77502fd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\pm 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545311} {:answer/id #uuid "58910719-15f1-5c10-aba5-c08657d568a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\pm 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545366} {:answer/id #uuid "5d867956-1afa-545a-8d5e-158bee736d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\pm \\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545377} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee5e500-cf1f-5e17-88ce-c0068943b54e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\pm \\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545492} {:answer/id #uuid "dae9e2a6-948b-5b95-a02c-61c1a8d0f2ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d867956-1afa-545a-8d5e-158bee736d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\pm \\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545377} :answer-field/id #uuid "2138cdc1-7319-514a-94f4-c743a1b5525e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "af7384d0-ffc3-51cc-833d-b329ee1b978c" :question/math-academy-id "q-111429" :question/problem "Solve for $z$ where $3{z}^{2}-\\frac{25}{12}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the ${z}^{2}$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-\\frac{25}{12} & =0 \\\\ 3{z}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{12} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\ |z| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{25}}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\ |z| & =\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ z & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a779e3c0-db96-5a84-bf30-5894050e9878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a779e3c0-db96-5a84-bf30-5894050e9878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546626} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecbd6582-13a5-5dd4-8f0c-314a10cf5dbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e7a7bc64-476c-516f-9ef3-e891c084febe" :question/math-academy-id "q-218571" :question/problem "If $81{a}^{2}=16,$ then $a=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the ${a}^{2}$ on one side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}81{a}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {a}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the square root of both sides of the equation and solve the resulting absolute value equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{a}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{81}} \\\\ |a| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ |a| & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ a & =\\pm \\frac{4}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074afea5-fe43-5f97-a337-7ba0b62f6b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.19\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557576} {:answer/id #uuid "3a34b986-628b-586a-a824-0c50dc501cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48.86\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557589} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2bd5f4-6495-521f-86b4-02f859db90d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557602} {:answer/id #uuid "abf2cb4e-eaec-5478-bf8c-311b280dee6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.53\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557611} {:answer/id #uuid "c8adf232-a644-562e-a8a4-c1c1bb1a72e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.20\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b2bd5f4-6495-521f-86b4-02f859db90d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557602} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cf78f28-d615-5f34-b6af-c0a3d54ad644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0f49ce74-136e-52d1-86ad-43f235e9acf3" :question/problem "A fish hatchery tank initially holds $560\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved salt.\nA feed solution containing $60\\,\\text{g/L}$ of the salt is pumped into the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of salt in the tank $2$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 60\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the salt enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =2.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $560\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.4-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=2.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =2.4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =33.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=33.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+33.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=16,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =16 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+33.6 & =16 \\\\ A & =-17.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-t/14}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of salt in the tank $2$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(2)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-2/14}≈18.34\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77e46878-fee9-50ca-98ed-c5b7828d3a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.84\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557601} {:answer/id #uuid "cf838e6d-1539-51ea-a41d-a097421083cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.80\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557616} {:answer/id #uuid "dc34f15f-1945-5fa3-8396-6a623c0a9b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557620} {:answer/id #uuid "f1df0184-1f89-5672-8d0f-455e36d369e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.88\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557622} {:answer/id #uuid "fca55ac1-6715-59f4-9c7b-dcca6d1300f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.87\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc34f15f-1945-5fa3-8396-6a623c0a9b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557620} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bd3035f-bf25-5206-aea4-72cd5a9c6c81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "166e03f1-5265-5464-911b-78beb96ade57" :question/problem "A dye vat initially holds $900\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye is pumped into the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of dye in the vat $5$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of dye in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the vat minus the rate at which dye leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye enters the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the vat.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the vat remains $900\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}Q=0.9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{60}\\alpha =0.9\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=54.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60}+54.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/60}+54 & =15 \\\\ A & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=54-39{e}^{-t/60}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of dye in the vat $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=54-39{e}^{-5/60}≈18.12\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32982bee-e49c-5d3c-9ef9-9797891a4a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.80\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557586} {:answer/id #uuid "4db1f4c8-9663-5522-85a7-ac3f6dbb587a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557594} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb7d840-bfd4-5d23-a40a-54e9d62eb633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.88\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557603} {:answer/id #uuid "8c234248-4412-52b3-a150-1ca22bcf39ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.87\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557607} {:answer/id #uuid "d70ad977-02f6-5ad7-a6df-586c35f0ecd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.84\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4db1f4c8-9663-5522-85a7-ac3f6dbb587a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.12\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557594} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f0d8da9-229e-5e9e-b1b3-e1ac54f4e6c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "25f7192c-e0d8-5fe5-81fc-4a226077ebf0" :question/problem "A dye vat initially holds $900\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA feed solution containing $60\\,\\text{g/L}$ of the dye is pumped into the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of dye in the vat $5$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 60\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of dye in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the vat minus the rate at which dye leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye enters the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the vat.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the vat remains $900\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}Q=0.9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{60}\\alpha =0.9\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=54.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60}+54.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $15\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=15,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =15 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/60}+54 & =15 \\\\ A & =-39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=54-39{e}^{-t/60}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of dye in the vat $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=54-39{e}^{-5/60}≈18.12\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557581 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c1d1dad-63e4-53b7-84e8-513185e6d304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.19\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557582} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6bca90-9a03-5e51-b918-70f707c6acd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557583} {:answer/id #uuid "3519affe-7179-51a9-9bfa-a2371788e71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.20\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557587} {:answer/id #uuid "41d54f50-31ef-5861-8452-ba4837b9997d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.53\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557592} {:answer/id #uuid "97fcfedc-afd7-5fbd-b34c-7c2e83880c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48.86\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c6bca90-9a03-5e51-b918-70f707c6acd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557583} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc00a72d-8bd3-57d1-9224-3683c4e00277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "296bf566-e278-5d2e-983b-7265ee8fce14" :question/problem "A fish hatchery tank initially holds $560\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved salt.\nA feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the salt is pumped into the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of salt in the tank $2$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.06\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the salt enters the tank at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.06\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =2.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $560\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $40\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(40\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(40\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40Q}{560}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{14}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.4-\\frac{Q}{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}Q=2.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{14}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{14}\\alpha =2.4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =33.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=33.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/14}+33.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $16\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=16,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =16 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/14}+33.6 & =16 \\\\ A & =-17.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-t/14}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of salt in the tank $2$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(2)=33.6-17.6{e}^{-2/14}≈18.34\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecd4a70-5d43-5b6e-b51f-7860ae3003f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.48\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557591} {:answer/id #uuid "53fa8896-959f-5e2c-bef6-40a7e49e0d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.29\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557595} {:answer/id #uuid "7505055c-ebf8-59d8-95d5-613b69c1b664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.72\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557600} {:answer/id #uuid "802b7168-0e5a-518c-9174-74d6fc00f09f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25.16\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557605} {:answer/id #uuid "c754b326-0708-5675-b4f9-bc4175d982b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.08\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7505055c-ebf8-59d8-95d5-613b69c1b664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.72\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557600} :answer-field/id #uuid "e946d965-efc2-5c52-804f-d33d0dbc4425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "45703f0e-7f20-5a04-876b-6489f25e25bd" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $720\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $24\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive.\nA feed solution containing $30\\,\\text{g/L}$ of the additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of additive in the tank $3$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 30\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additive enters the tank minus the rate at which additives leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additive enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additive exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $720\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{720}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{24}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{24}Q=\\frac{9}{10}.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{24}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/24},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{24}\\alpha =\\frac{9}{10}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =21.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=21.6.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/24}+21.6.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $24\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=24,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =24 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/24}+21.6 & =24 \\\\ A & =2.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=21.6+2.4{e}^{-t/24}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of additive in the tank $3$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(3)=21.6+2.4{e}^{-3/24}≈23.72\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb044d0-3b14-5394-8670-15805bd24f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.77\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557579} {:answer/id #uuid "2fcc7f0d-605a-57f5-a1d3-53972e15f66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.39\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557585} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4e95dc-9cb4-5ea0-9b47-f9315553f561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.62\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557590} {:answer/id #uuid "83a9bfca-6560-5ddc-9727-d1da746b19e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.27\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557606} {:answer/id #uuid "b20b695b-e151-57d5-aa83-88aa35a655c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.23\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20b695b-e151-57d5-aa83-88aa35a655c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.23\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557612} :answer-field/id #uuid "9691fa90-f342-5762-97c2-78f160bf5d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "66ef737b-6897-576f-afdf-4557492d2e08" :question/problem "A coolant tank initially holds $625\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $13\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive.\nA feed solution containing $30\\,\\text{g/L}$ of the additive is pumped into the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of additive in the tank $6$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 30\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of additive in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which additive enters the tank minus the rate at which additive leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additive enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the additive enters the tank at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *additive* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.75\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additive exits the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $625\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of additive in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{625}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $25\\,\\text{L/min},$ *additive* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(25\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{625}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{625}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25Q}{625}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.75-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=0.75.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{25}\\alpha =0.75\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =18.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=18.75.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25}+18.75.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $13\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved additive. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=13,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =13 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/25}+18.75 & =13 \\\\ A & =-5.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=18.75-5.75{e}^{-t/25}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of additive in the tank $6$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(6)=18.75-5.75{e}^{-6/25}≈14.23\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557610 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eab0c72-a9b6-5914-a862-8b14af356b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.68\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557584} {:answer/id #uuid "36655816-75f6-5463-8b00-0f2116bceb0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23.73\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557588} {:answer/id #uuid "71feb9bd-395e-5d1e-a142-a57c5b2eba4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.05\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557599} {:answer/id #uuid "d557c772-fa39-54f8-84d9-854c4e0af932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45.95\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557617} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e97afd-028a-57f8-b966-b25ee6c663ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27.29\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71feb9bd-395e-5d1e-a142-a57c5b2eba4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26.05\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557599} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb724058-9e20-5567-9da6-1e400ea9f683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9970f4fb-cac3-5bd9-8a36-546b0cf184df" :question/problem "A dye vat initially holds $900\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $25\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA feed solution containing $40\\,\\text{g/L}$ of the dye is pumped into the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of dye in the vat $6$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "The feed concentration must use kilograms: $$\n\\displaystyle 40\\,\\text{g/L}\\cdot\\frac{1\\,\\text{kg}}{1000\\,\\text{g}}=0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nLet $Q$ be the amount of dye in the vat at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the vat minus the rate at which dye leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the vat.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the dye enters the vat at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the vat.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the vat remains $900\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the vat, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{900}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{60}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{60}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=36.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/60}+36.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $25\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/60}+36 & =25 \\\\ A & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=36-11{e}^{-t/60}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of dye in the vat $6$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(6)=36-11{e}^{-6/60}≈26.05\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "300fad0e-fd42-55a8-89ea-fd53b94738a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564862} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb4e3d9-c68d-5d96-b2ca-e83a0ec8f365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eb4e3d9-c68d-5d96-b2ca-e83a0ec8f365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564887} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a756679-ef95-5ead-ac0c-59325a180803" :answer-field/key "At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564848} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a00f9c7c-eb2e-534f-96d9-21f5d55d8e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564893} {:answer/id #uuid "e51414ea-9ddc-56ea-b7da-1ed3610556b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e51414ea-9ddc-56ea-b7da-1ed3610556b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564918} :answer-field/id #uuid "6995f8c6-a759-5950-9349-963ca5de09e3" :answer-field/key "At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564875} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "758c0564-deae-52c8-b5b3-c47827255183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564881} {:answer/id #uuid "b707bcbb-75bb-5476-bbd6-45f627b422ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "758c0564-deae-52c8-b5b3-c47827255183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564881} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa5f12c-832f-57ec-a592-7f5244b29e13" :answer-field/key "At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "02cbb13a-a6d7-5c1c-8f40-eee3ed956640" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-9x$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=-9x.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0.$ In each open quadrant, $x$ has a fixed nonzero sign. The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/83/8306880f3f6b3d936d1e690cf75eb9e1a999f5b1c914d10c1886296317664fbe.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27375c71-a245-5b89-8301-e80128b59e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564860} {:answer/id #uuid "af100ac0-69da-54d8-b84e-0d83930a7923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af100ac0-69da-54d8-b84e-0d83930a7923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564895} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c67c4ae-4832-5229-bb20-58d7731d261b" :answer-field/key "At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564868} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60136280-1f12-504b-9683-e3565873d680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564874} {:answer/id #uuid "9009cb1f-03a7-5f91-a9f0-dd1794c7994f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9009cb1f-03a7-5f91-a9f0-dd1794c7994f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564888} :answer-field/id #uuid "c10fbfae-0c80-51ab-b65c-a6d891652af6" :answer-field/key "At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564904} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e25e2e33-2c89-5fcb-b8c3-ed3090c4d18c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564915} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4a105b-ceb3-5369-a285-0eb8ccce9659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef4a105b-ceb3-5369-a285-0eb8ccce9659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564920} :answer-field/id #uuid "f521ac1c-ccfd-5b5e-851c-0d50cbcbf7a4" :answer-field/key "At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0310b6c3-7a71-5e78-8f0c-a9029c12ce0c" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIn an open quadrant, each squared coordinate is positive, so the remaining coordinate factors and coefficient determine the sign.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ec462e2ce1055ca352de84813bc6d72731d74f93d44a1513b1dc284b504cb0b.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592