$ In each open quadrant, $x$ has a fixed nonzero sign. The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ec462e2ce1055ca352de84813bc6d72731d74f93d44a1513b1dc284b504cb0b.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215906ac-156c-5760-86d2-bded7b124e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564855} {:answer/id #uuid "8663cab0-7b61-591d-860b-3d377d745d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8663cab0-7b61-591d-860b-3d377d745d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564886} :answer-field/id #uuid "13726f73-133b-5735-9c96-f19b6e2e23b8" :answer-field/key "At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564852} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5863989b-ab50-5aa0-b50c-395a1508f380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564873} {:answer/id #uuid "b270c1c0-fd88-5ee7-b891-c4adc10c237c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b270c1c0-fd88-5ee7-b891-c4adc10c237c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564898} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f4872b3-cf14-5403-9ec3-b650816f4f82" :answer-field/key "At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564877} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "291e70ec-7bcf-5eac-a794-b4c344111aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564861} {:answer/id #uuid "bf95cd42-6157-5534-b883-f42ed631d0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf95cd42-6157-5534-b883-f42ed631d0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564903} :answer-field/id #uuid "b205df35-2a41-5cf5-af10-5e46bf456a1b" :answer-field/key "At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f3c5f9ee-c923-5bed-81c7-8afd53970e0b" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{2}y$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIn an open quadrant, each squared coordinate is positive, so the remaining coordinate factors and coefficient determine the sign.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/b1/b151d959cb74133709e15f9a78b377988609bf761c174ca82f15fcf1d8d3d74c.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative”; “At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive”; “At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative”."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48848976-5af9-59a1-a8d5-7f34db74d877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565043} {:answer/id #uuid "77866715-4a00-54a6-9bca-78618431fd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565118} {:answer/id #uuid "984fc114-e07e-535b-bbfd-cf1625fa9db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565168} {:answer/id #uuid "c27ee232-913c-501f-8f38-24c7f5cfaae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565232} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d96702-504b-5f4f-9d1f-1e62ddfecd8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77866715-4a00-54a6-9bca-78618431fd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565118} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c29978b-66bd-5fe3-92c9-25071bfcad0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3e84a1e0-ab07-582e-a187-ce57adaa478b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74961" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}}=\\frac{1-3+2}{\\sqrt{3-2}-\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{y-x+z}{\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x-z}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x-z}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x-z})}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{(y-x+z)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}\\,\\frac{-(x-z-y)(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)}{x-z-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (3,1,2)}-(\\sqrt{x-z}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{3-2}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2776b54f-1b5d-5a24-9264-60188b98a30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565001} {:answer/id #uuid "47a3084a-dea7-5252-a052-f22a8d9afbb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565041} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2058cc-d3ee-5a9d-abaa-0c2aab0fc895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565157} {:answer/id #uuid "91e130a9-184e-5357-91fa-a31e71ffed64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565159} {:answer/id #uuid "9b09b8cb-03b3-5495-a712-64af8592a360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2776b54f-1b5d-5a24-9264-60188b98a30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565001} :answer-field/id #uuid "a63c1194-144a-5227-ac9c-0935b59c5eb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "416deefa-43e7-520e-bca5-f8da057711d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74967" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=0$ (the $x$-axis),  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c334b8-5f64-5024-8be8-3440026a135f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564995} {:answer/id #uuid "b3785525-f411-5114-943e-5a2cf050adfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565205} {:answer/id #uuid "b689f50d-16ad-525a-aff0-c88e838473ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565212} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cccb62-ae0c-505e-946a-32fd9dfe1c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565241} {:answer/id #uuid "d4522578-3775-5aa6-9639-2a945876ec85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7cccb62-ae0c-505e-946a-32fd9dfe1c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565241} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1873304-acce-598c-9592-a96cff0a14c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "585a757a-6bf9-546a-9bf9-80289d848f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-74968" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=-x$,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{xy}{2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48c22d73-cf17-502a-b856-207b42d6d523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565045} {:answer/id #uuid "649ec646-7b0f-548e-9c35-08857d9dbb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565093} {:answer/id #uuid "804fbbe3-5623-55ba-bc08-0b6088ac245d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565135} {:answer/id #uuid "b490877c-e83c-59f4-a1c9-d3fa593a9f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565210} {:answer/id #uuid "ed15ba70-79a7-5e28-8379-b7e2a244dd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "804fbbe3-5623-55ba-bc08-0b6088ac245d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565135} :answer-field/id #uuid "eecb02cb-44a7-5766-9e3d-1d6dd4f8028f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6491bac7-604f-53bc-80e6-378575a48cb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-129324" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x-y+3.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-2,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-2,0)}(2x-y+3) & =2(-2)-0+3 \\\\  & =-4-0+3 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f47eef-aa78-50a5-b976-e93df5f4c292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564942} {:answer/id #uuid "41b96b77-5e73-5c67-af13-27ac8b7eb13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565034} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a30f22-3124-51ba-913f-5a068a49c15a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565237} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bef289-3178-5ae8-94bb-0bc7078744bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565261} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c561d0-62a5-54e4-bf55-93094dadec30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41b96b77-5e73-5c67-af13-27ac8b7eb13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565034} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3985035-b9b2-56db-8d94-fdc34f531158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cbddc293-6a3c-5b64-88ba-c447f360dc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129347" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\frac{9-({1}^{2})({3}^{2})}{\\sqrt{9}-1(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-9}{3-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x}-yz} & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{(\\sqrt{x}-yz)(\\sqrt{x}+yz)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)}{\\cancel{x-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (9,1,3)}(\\sqrt{x}+yz) & =\\sqrt{9}+1(3) \\\\  & =3+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116e62d7-9676-5ce3-b68b-c1b2b15289ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564966} {:answer/id #uuid "1c785494-6256-5978-8b37-733bf1a00a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564980} {:answer/id #uuid "55deb21b-ba9f-58dd-91dc-0aad1e32f9a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565065} {:answer/id #uuid "b04bbf06-0a8f-5897-8e52-3272aed01886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565200} {:answer/id #uuid "f3957818-b350-5503-95cc-19f562857757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3957818-b350-5503-95cc-19f562857757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565306} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7ebaa1a-8a6b-5d7a-acd1-3db045338942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ec0d423c-ac59-5358-a37f-88f2016b24ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-65138" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{(-1)^{3}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by factoring the numerator and denominator and then canceling common factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy+xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x+y)+y(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)+y(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(2x+y)(x+y)}{({x}^{2}+y)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2x+y}{{x}^{2}+y} & =\\frac{2(-1)+(1)}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567046 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37dd99bc-3607-581f-978c-a71b67914c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}-2{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566477} {:answer/id #uuid "9126a86d-bcfd-5fec-9571-5503eba42ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566648} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d265ad-a1a6-58b0-b6d6-e96a59278366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}}-\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566688} {:answer/id #uuid "dafdca6c-9141-5612-9f79-f5c98e819afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566803} {:answer/id #uuid "f64dcd14-9eac-5db3-bba6-758aa5bc23bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}}{4}-\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{6}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9126a86d-bcfd-5fec-9571-5503eba42ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566648} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1056adf-eded-5bde-8128-44705a2baad4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "676d4494-4f46-573f-a890-2a8daa607201" :question/math-academy-id "q-113850" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of \n$\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}-x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}-x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int {x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}-\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{(1+1)}+2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{(-4+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-3}}{(-3)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67ebaae1-1b6f-54a0-9ee3-20ad6d94b297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-{x}^{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566561} {:answer/id #uuid "75869535-6c65-5fee-b563-b5239aaa4910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566587} {:answer/id #uuid "949d1af7-fd39-55bc-bf6f-81a0c846d362" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566654} {:answer/id #uuid "a1102e39-908b-5d4b-9cc6-16a7726229bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566676} {:answer/id #uuid "e18db342-254e-56c4-b18d-3e730f819b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1102e39-908b-5d4b-9cc6-16a7726229bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566676} :answer-field/id #uuid "abc76652-94ff-55a2-98b8-14e731f2dc71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9e4827b1-c8af-5e0e-a92c-bab0c6f19c7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71167" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 5-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\left(\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}\\right)-\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C \\\\  & =5x-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06327c3c-dce0-563c-b02b-f7d69ae0503a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}{5}+\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566371} {:answer/id #uuid "56759450-adc6-5b0d-a635-c7f96a6a0550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566525} {:answer/id #uuid "86252f96-8aa6-54b2-b5a8-c3649023555d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566616} {:answer/id #uuid "98759795-857b-56c3-8fe8-c710268b79fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}{2}+3\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566661} {:answer/id #uuid "ec410b0b-b1e5-506b-ad48-150967b9799d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}{5}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06327c3c-dce0-563c-b02b-f7d69ae0503a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}{5}+\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566371} :answer-field/id #uuid "dea66fd9-ec24-52e5-9e8a-c08de6b5d949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa2d5333-814d-52c3-9ed1-e83660135b41" :question/math-academy-id "q-49063" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{x}(2+x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}(2+x)\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}(2+x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{x}^{1/2}+{x}^{3/2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {x}^{1/2}\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{3/2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}{5}+\\frac{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3179ff10-c8be-5032-b116-17b3d443db6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{7}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566460} {:answer/id #uuid "51d8327b-b62f-54ce-a7a1-472117a5b25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566520} {:answer/id #uuid "52a5c30a-6d06-54eb-a7f8-2ebe257c1193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{7}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566522} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef5f0ad-1feb-54d4-9d4a-009b294fb168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{7}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566572} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcbdf16-64b9-50b1-9679-0fa5d22435fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}-\\frac{7}{{x}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3179ff10-c8be-5032-b116-17b3d443db6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{7}{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566460} :answer-field/id #uuid "69ab0297-0b7d-5f64-9cc7-f805d6068f4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c3b5e67d-32a5-5a89-860d-1eeac5fbca6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49059" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{5}+7}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{5}+7}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{3}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 7{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+7\\int {x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)+7\\left(\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{7}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571385 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7864c3b-b764-56cd-a641-bd65ea7dd484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec^{2}(x)\\log(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7864c3b-b764-56cd-a641-bd65ea7dd484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec^{2}(x)\\log(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571265} :answer-field/id #uuid "0af8eb9f-fc24-5d73-ad75-f548e3ea3646" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "303e2b76-22b6-55f6-abfa-1986d9ab5f9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-320820" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\operatorname{log}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n**Hint:** $\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)={log}_{10}⁡(x)$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))}^{′}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x){(\\operatorname{log}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡(x))(\\operatorname{log}⁡(x))+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\right) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa1cb47c-2e18-5935-a199-4342ba479343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa1cb47c-2e18-5935-a199-4342ba479343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571271} :answer-field/id #uuid "99aa048f-795f-5835-b3e4-7ab1bb44a8a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a0fee3ca-7f95-5f0e-a414-4282e409bf7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-319824" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=2x+1$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)+1}{1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fbb081d-8fbf-5577-b042-226064e10be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571014} {:answer/id #uuid "a20a9a53-3e6f-5754-8799-d24e521ac2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571145} {:answer/id #uuid "a462a7c9-b56d-5b3e-b5f2-01b27202da04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571149} {:answer/id #uuid "cadbcaa0-5f4b-5598-a6eb-4541c20a3865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571202} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2fd5e5-1d32-56a9-b43f-1421f5392621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a462a7c9-b56d-5b3e-b5f2-01b27202da04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571149} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d091bc-16c4-5784-a6c4-28309a12be4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d8004d91-2319-5283-80d1-a0670d83fdc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104300" :question/problem "Differentiate ${x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={x}^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+({x}^{3})(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
